[[:db/retract 17592186316829 :answer-field/choices 17592186315955] [:db/retract 17592186316982 :answer-field/choices 17592186315740] [:db/retract 17592186316971 :answer-field/choices 17592186315264] [:db/retract 17592186316679 :answer-field/choices 17592186315126] [:db/retract 17592186317040 :answer-field/choices 17592186315769] [:db/retract 17592186316738 :answer-field/choices 17592186316014] [:db/retract 17592186316654 :answer-field/choices 17592186315478] [:db/retract 17592186316842 :answer-field/choices 17592186315800] {:db/id 17592186221449 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c4b9a1ad-55e6-52d3-8f59-ef1cc199427a" "question-6b957da1-0df2-5626-98d6-1a7c5a3632dc" "question-46096919-32d4-552d-ad62-e0e1723dd83b" "question-3df34aa2-66f8-53fb-b84c-e23ff9464f43"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221201 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a41865c9f4c1ea90ac559af16948e7af546a4ad3842adf954f4d8227c3583ab6.svg)\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x \\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\nI. $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nII. $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\nIII. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\nIV. $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221450 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fa5cac21-63bb-59e7-aa34-0b7a399b8755"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221200 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function\n$F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$\ndefined on $[-5,5).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=5x$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](images/64/64e7a907f9d039e0b8a9ff71ba4bab18a9325a8322552cda69391e239bf3339f.svg)\nSince $f$ is ![](images/9c/9c95f62579d17c626e85bd76ba0028b378e8a0a3c2b9879d8e596bd9773eea84.svg)\nand  ![](images/90/90d690278324e4611fb59fa60aae82457d5ee10e595057bdd9d9f0e8a6a9db93.svg)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](images/68/689e50221843f953a679c91852b2cc11b4b89cb8aa86613be59fcec65ac75c43.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221451 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a8bfe2ba-a961-5858-9456-3382dda97cd5" "question-e9666624-6b3b-5d5b-bd5e-3f9ae5e53130" "question-3c9cd435-4b0a-516c-bede-18c713c259dc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221202 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{4}x^2$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}x^3 = \\int_0^x f(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](images/ca/cad855e780f39df95e18c8c89e1994a1f60e5609bae77be32d779c2a37dfb7c5.svg)\n Since $f$ is \n![](images/ec/ec188620716be3a7ba759589ec331eb4a2b0b8c522b45382f535889d1c980760.svg)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](images/62/627f5788ec8cf06c5cf32245f8128edba2de2d5941f1e4ffcdc61645ab97c27d.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4b9a1ad-55e6-52d3-8f59-ef1cc199427a" :question/id #uuid "c4b9a1ad-55e6-52d3-8f59-ef1cc199427a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342459" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/11f9718c9384ad2fea0bf3f5cc7da720559c512a5b3cebbc11ca966d5ec0f28b.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}2x=2(-3)=-6\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0212ece76aba288e88b0c4d9f2e0f6163f4fd1f594cee30e9ea5f764ebc5f69.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f816e4e-cb4e-5a7e-a240-55ae635308bb" "answer-field-87c2f9ef-7400-51b3-adc8-5976e8bc684f" "answer-field-f6f9f671-11c9-5940-b954-9ff8e3b7a115" "answer-field-dfc29204-c5ea-57f3-b73f-d309a805b808"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f816e4e-cb4e-5a7e-a240-55ae635308bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f816e4e-cb4e-5a7e-a240-55ae635308bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-0cf9be56-efa9-5355-8976-ae2a23fa9c57" :answer-field/choices ["answer-518e2251-cc5d-5108-bce8-18685a7d6e9a" "answer-0cf9be56-efa9-5355-8976-ae2a23fa9c57"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-518e2251-cc5d-5108-bce8-18685a7d6e9a" :answer/id #uuid "518e2251-cc5d-5108-bce8-18685a7d6e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "False" :db/ens